<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">League of the Public Weal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1465">1465</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The League of the Public Weal was an alliance of great French nobles formed in 1465 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, led by Charles of Charolais (later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>), Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> of Brittany, Duke John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> and the king's brother Charles of Berry. After the indecisive Battle of Montlhéry, Louis made large concessions in the treaties of Conflans and Saint-Maur, which he soon undermined.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal.jpg" alt="League of the Public Weal" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circle of Étienne Colaud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerre_du_Bien_public.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Like earlier Capetian and Valois kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> asserted royal supremacy throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, continuing an administrative unification that had advanced through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and the nobles of the league sought to restore their feudal prerogatives. Its moving spirit was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, Count of Charolais, heir to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, whose lands included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and the Imperial territories of Holland and Brabant. He hoped to make Burgundy independent of France as a kingdom between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, reviving the ancient Lotharingia. The king's brother Charles, Duke of Berry, served as figurehead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and the people of Liège, and with the support of Gaston IV, Count of Foix, he raised an army of 30,000. In May and June 1465 he attacked the Bourbonnais, then raced the Breton and Burgundian armies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He met the Burgundian army under Charolais at Montlhéry, near Longpont-sur-Orge, on 16 July 1465. Both sides claimed victory: Charolais held the field, while the king withdrew by night to Paris, though his uncle the Count of Maine had fled with a third of the royal troops.</p><p>On 19 July the Burgundian and Breton armies joined, soon followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-armagnac">Counts of Armagnac</a> and Albret and the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and the princes besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Louis left on 10 August, gathered reinforcements and provisions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and returned on 28 August. A truce of 3 September did not stop the leaguers from taking Pontoise and Rouen, and Louis chose to appear to yield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the treaties of Conflans (5 October), Saint-Maur (29 October) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> (23 December 1465), Charles of Berry received Normandy, Charolais the Somme towns, John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> lordships, 300 lances and 100,000 crowns, Jacques of Armagnac the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, Count of Saint-Pol, the office of constable; a committee of 36 under Dunois was to reform abuses. Louis did not keep the terms for long. In March 1466 Charles lost Normandy and fled to Brittany, and a new league of 1468 broke apart when Louis, backed by the States General at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, detached Brittany and his brother by the Treaty of Ancenis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Public Weal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Francis II, who had no son, joined the <b>League of the Public Weal</b> against Louis XI in 1465 and later sheltered princes in revolt against the regency of Anne of Beaujeu, giving the crown reason to intervene.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
